  "criteria": [
    {
      "detail": "every series' log mix shifted by its own constant, 969 series refitted: worst change in the slope 8.632e-15. This is what lets the reading run on a file that carries no cross-country level: the shift lands entirely in the intercept.",
      "kind": "instrument",
      "name": "B101-9  the fitted slope does not move when the index base moves",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "counts and objects printed, no threshold on an estimator. Both sides of the ratio are built from half samples so the null is exactly one: across the countries of an industry, the spread of the first-half slope over the spread of (first minus second) over root two. Across 55 industries with at least 5 countries the ratio runs 0.24 to 3.62, median 0.91, and 25 of 55 sit above one and 1 above three. The same statistic on each country's SECOND half gives median 0.79, which is the check that the split itself is not driving it. Reading fixed in advance: the numerator and the denominator are the same statistic on the same countries, so one relation per industry puts the ratio at one; the middle state is where it sits inside a factor of three either way, and that is where it sits. p10 to p90 across industries is 0.55 to 1.58. **Undecided, and undecided is the reading, not a failure**: the country-specific slope the paper fits is not distinguishable here from a country's own slope moving over time. Slope range inside an industry, widest three: G46 [-0.343, +0.252], H49 [-0.402, +0.374], H51 [-0.189, +0.197]",
      "kind": "own_reading",
      "name": "B101-10  how far the share-mix slope spreads across countries inside an industry, which is their own restriction and not their country term",
      "passed": null,
      "void": true
    },
    {
      "cite": "Griffith, Redding & Van Reenen 2004, Review of Economics and Statistics 86(4): 883-895, the production function and the share-smoothing paragraph",
      "detail": "Void: their point prediction is that this ratio is one, and it is 0.91, so on this panel it holds. That is a reading about their production function and it is recorded as one. It is NOT scored, because this framework makes no competing prediction about the slope of a factor share on a factor ratio: its object is the terms two holders get at one position, and the share-mix slope is not that object. A first version of this criterion wrote one in so that a cell could be filled, which is the same error a station earlier in this line made and withdrew the same day. Median ratio 0.91; 25 of 55 industries above one, 1 above three.",
      "grid": "unread",
      "grid_note": {
        "B": "not claimed: nothing of this framework's was read into any cell here, so the four-cell table is untouched. What was read is that their own stated restriction on the slope holds. It does NOT narrow where the non-integrability lives: their country term is the intercept, and on a cross-country loop a common slope contributes exactly zero while the intercept contributes all of it. A first version of this note claimed that narrowing, and the claim rested on two swapped subscripts."
      },
      "kind": "rival",
      "name": "B101-11  one production function per industry-year gives one slope",
      "passed": null,
      "rival_entailment": "a slope that varies across industries alone carries no country variation, so its spread across the countries of one industry is estimation noise and matches a floor built from each country's own two halves",
      "rival_premise": "footnote 20 prints sigma_ijt = alpha_ij + beta_j ln(K/L), and the text allows the coefficient on the capital-labour ratio to vary across industries j, so the slope carries no country",
      "rival_shape": "point prediction",
      "void": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "Void: not evaluated, and it cannot be on this carrier. With sigma = alpha_ij + beta_j ln(K/L) and beta common inside an industry, the holonomy of a cross-country loop is exactly the area the intercept sweeps: the slope term is affine and common, so it telescopes. The observed log mix is the true one plus a constant fixed per series, and a fit on it returns the slope untouched while the intercept comes back as alpha - beta * e. So B101-9, that the slope is blind to the base, is precisely why the intercept is not. The cross-country half needs a 2015 cross-section, and this is the criterion that says why.",
      "kind": "premise",
      "name": "B101-18  the coefficient the cross-country reading needs is the one this file destroys",
      "passed": null,
      "void": true
    },
